    "name": "free-day-min-the-averaging-multiplies-the-window-and-the-requirement",
    "why": "'One free day in two, averaged over two windows' is 'at least two free days in any four consecutive days' — BOTH numbers move, and an implementation that multiplied only the window would ask for one free day in four, which is a materially weaker rule wearing the same parameters. p-ali works the 1st to the 3rd, so the four days from the 1st hold a single free day and fail while the four from the 2nd hold exactly two and pass: the boundary sits between two adjacent windows, where multiplying only one of the two numbers moves it. The case also exercises the floor's own gate — the three windows reaching back before the evaluable range are left unjudged and REPORTED rather than counted short.",
